@page "/blogs/getting-started-with-aspire-in-dotnet"
@using SharedModels
@using BaseComponents
@inherits BasePage

<Content FileName=@nameof(GettingStartedWithAspireInDotNet)>
    <ContentBody>
        <What>
            <p>
                A new developer should not need a map of terminals just to start the application.
                <Highlight>Aspire</Highlight> gives an existing distributed system one
                code-defined <Highlight>AppHost</Highlight> that describes its services,
                databases, tools, and connections, then starts them together.
            </p>
            <p>
                This article shows how to add that orchestration layer to an existing production
                application, what <Highlight>ServiceDefaults</Highlight> contributes, and how to
                use the dashboard, database resources, and isolated worktree runs. It focuses on
                application wiring; your services keep their existing business logic.
            </p>
        </What>

        <Why>
            <p>
                Starting a system with many services by hand means remembering container runtimes,
                environment settings, startup order, and which terminal owns each process. In a
                real application, a local graph may contain around <Highlight>30 resources</Highlight>:
                projects, databases, message brokers, user interfaces, and supporting tools.
                That count describes a development graph, not 30 containers or a production rollout.
            </p>
            <p>
                Recreating that graph for every teammate makes onboarding slow and local setups
                inconsistent. A shared AppHost gives developers and AI coding agents an
                end-to-end view of the system, while one launch and one dashboard replace the
                startup checklist.
            </p>
            <p>
                Stale data also makes a clean checkout behave differently from a long-lived
                workstation. A fresh database resource plus the application's existing migration
                and seed process gives each new local run a predictable baseline; Aspire itself
                does not reset or seed business data. Point tests at disposable local resources;
                Aspire does not prevent an AppHost from connecting to shared staging data.
            </p>
        </Why>

        <How>
            <h4>1. Initialize Aspire in the solution you already have</h4>
            <p>
                Use the existing solution's required .NET SDK, a running container runtime, and
                the Aspire CLI. If the CLI is not installed, the .NET global tool is one supported
                option:
            </p>
            <CodeSnippet CssClass="language-powershell">
dotnet tool install -g Aspire.Cli
aspire --version
            </CodeSnippet>
            <p>
                Run the version command to verify installation, then clone or open the existing
                solution and follow its local configuration process. Keep credentials in the
                team's approved local secret store, not in source files.
            </p>
            <CodeSnippet CssClass="language-powershell">
aspire init --language csharp
            </CodeSnippet>
            <p>
                In a <Highlight>.sln</Highlight> or <Highlight>.slnx</Highlight> workspace,
                <Highlight>aspire init</Highlight> scaffolds a project-based AppHost with project
                references and strongly typed <Highlight>AddProject</Highlight> calls for solution
                projects. It establishes the orchestration entry point; it does not infer every
                application-specific resource, dependency, or service setting.
            </p>
            <p>
                Add an infrastructure integration with the CLI instead of hand-editing package
                files. This adds PostgreSQL hosting support to the AppHost; it does not create
                application data:
            </p>
            <CodeSnippet CssClass="language-powershell">
aspire add postgres
            </CodeSnippet>
            <p>
                If the CLI offers agent setup, enable it for your coding agent and ask it to run
                the installed <Highlight>aspireify</Highlight> skill. The skill can inspect the
                solution, ask you to confirm the proposed resources, wire dependencies, configure
                ServiceDefaults and telemetry, then smoke-test with
                <Highlight>aspire start</Highlight>. Review the proposed graph and settings
                before accepting them; initialization alone does not know every application
                contract.
            </p>

            <h4>2. Describe the existing production topology in the AppHost</h4>
            <p>
                An AppHost is a separate development-time process, not a new business service.
                Copy both blocks below into its entry point in order, replacing generated project
                types with the existing roles in your solution.
            </p>
            <CodeSnippet CssClass="language-csharp">
var builder = DistributedApplication.CreateBuilder(args);

var postgres = builder.AddPostgres("postgres")
    .WithImage("pgvector/pgvector", "pg16")
    .WithPgAdmin();

var identityDb = postgres.AddDatabase("identity-db");
var appDb = postgres.AddDatabase("application-db");

var identity = builder.AddProject&lt;Projects.IdentityService&gt;("identity")
    .WithReference(identityDb, "IdentityConnectionString")
    .WaitFor(identityDb);

var api = builder.AddProject&lt;Projects.Api&gt;("api")
    .WithReference(identityDb, "IdentityConnectionString")
    .WithReference(appDb, "CommandsConnectionString")
    .WithReference(appDb, "QueriesConnectionString")
    .WithReference(identity)
    .WaitFor(identity)
    .WaitFor(appDb);
            </CodeSnippet>
            <p>Part 2 connects the web and landing services to that graph:</p>
            <CodeSnippet CssClass="language-csharp">
var web = builder.AddProject&lt;Projects.Web&gt;("web")
    .WithReference(api)
    .WithReference(identity)
    .WaitFor(api)
    .WaitFor(identity);

builder.AddProject&lt;Projects.Landing&gt;("landing")
    .WaitFor(web);

await builder.Build().RunAsync();
            </CodeSnippet>
            <p>
                <Highlight>WithReference</Highlight> supplies service-discovery endpoints or a
                database connection string under the name the existing service expects.
                <Highlight>WaitFor</Highlight> declares startup dependencies; it waits for a
                dependency's health check when one is configured, or its running state otherwise.
                Add health checks that match each service's real readiness contract instead of
                treating a started process as proof that a business workflow works.
            </p>
            <p>
                The three connection-string names show how one database resource can preserve
                existing Identity, Commands, and Queries configuration. In the production-shaped
                graph, the AppHost derives OpenID Connect redirect and logout callbacks from the
                web HTTPS endpoint at runtime instead of hard-coding a machine port. Aspire's
                PostgreSQL connection properties can also be consumed by C#, TypeScript, Python,
                Go, and other apps using their own database clients.
            </p>

            <h4>3. Use ServiceDefaults for shared service configuration</h4>
            <p>
                <Highlight>ServiceDefaults</Highlight> is shared configuration used by participating
                application projects; the AppHost does not replace it. Aspire templates use it as
                a central place for OpenTelemetry logging, metrics and distributed traces,
                runtime and HTTP instrumentation, service discovery, and shared
                <Highlight>HttpClient</Highlight> defaults. The generated extension also provides
                conventions for <Highlight>/health</Highlight> and <Highlight>/alive</Highlight>.
            </p>
            <CodeSnippet CssClass="language-csharp">
var builder = WebApplication.CreateBuilder(args);
builder.AddServiceDefaults();

var app = builder.Build();
app.MapDefaultEndpoints();
            </CodeSnippet>
            <p>
                Keep the shared behavior consistent across services, then customize it centrally
                for your application. Defaults are not a promise that every production service
                uses identical health checks or retries: teams can disable or adjust particular
                registrations to match their reliability and readiness requirements. The
                production-shaped setup here customizes standard health and resilience
                registrations; the snippet shows common template integration points, not a claim
                that every template default is active in that system.
            </p>

            <h4>4. Add databases, brokers, and non-.NET resources as needed</h4>
            <p>
                Add integrations such as PostgreSQL or Kafka with the CLI, then model each
                resource and reference it from its consumers. The graph can also include
                non-.NET services, existing images, and administration tools. A broker reference
                supplies configuration and startup coordination; application code still owns
                migrations, seed data, message handling, and business behavior.
            </p>
            <p>
                For a new local run, use a session-lifetime database container without a persistent
                volume. The existing application startup or migration process should create its
                schema and seed the expected development data before the service reports ready.
                That gives a new AppHost run a clean baseline without asking Aspire to invent
                business data. A persistent volume or remote database keeps its state; Aspire does
                not clean it or guarantee isolation from shared environments.
            </p>

            <h4>5. Launch once, inspect the graph, and isolate worktrees</h4>
            <p>
                Run the AppHost from the solution or its project directory:
            </p>
            <CodeSnippet CssClass="language-powershell">
aspire run
            </CodeSnippet>
            <p>
                Aspire starts the modeled resources and opens its dashboard, where the team can
                inspect endpoints, health, logs, and traces together. In an IDE, choosing the
                AppHost as the startup project gives the same one-click local launch. Press
                Ctrl+C to stop the session and its managed resources; this is local orchestration,
                not a production deployment.
            </p>
            <p>
                For concurrent Git worktrees, start each instance with
                <Highlight>aspire run --isolated</Highlight>. Aspire randomizes dashboard and
                service endpoint ports, including configured predictable ports, and isolates
                instance user secrets. Application code and external tools that hard-code URLs,
                shared volumes or container names, and remote databases remain shared resources;
                configure those separately when worktrees must not affect each other.
            </p>
            <CodeSnippet CssClass="language-powershell">
aspire run --isolated
            </CodeSnippet>

