        </How>

        <Summary>
            <ul>
                <li>Initialize Aspire in the existing solution; keep service behavior and business code intact.</li>
                <li>The AppHost maps services, databases, dependencies, and runtime endpoints in one graph.</li>
                <li>ServiceDefaults centralizes shared observability, discovery, and service conventions.</li>
                <li>The application owns schema changes and seed data; new ephemeral database hosts provide a repeatable baseline.</li>
                <li>Use <Highlight>aspire run --isolated</Highlight> to work in parallel worktrees without managed port conflicts.</li>
                <li>Use the dashboard as a shared system map for developers and AI coding agents.</li>
            </ul>
            <p>
                <strong>What to read next:</strong> see
                <a class="underline" href="/blogs/distributed-testing-with-aspire-and-playwright-in-dotnet/">how to test an Aspire application across its UI and services</a>.
            </p>
        </Summary>
    </ContentBody>
